{
  "$schema": "../signalset.schema.json",
  "vehicle": "Ford-F-150-Hybrid-2021",
  "description": "Ford F-150 PowerBoost full hybrid, 2021 on. OBDb's F-150 data has NO hybrid-battery signals, so this set is two things: (1) the 12 V battery monitor and vehicle basics from OBDb's F-150 set, and (2) the HV battery signals Ford's hybrid battery controller (BECM 7E4) uses on the Maverick hybrid, Mustang Mach-E and F-150 Lightning, taken from OBDb's Maverick set and marked UNCONFIRMED: nobody has reported them from a PowerBoost. 11-bit CAN, UDS 0x22 reads only. NOT read on a vehicle by this project.",
  "provenance": {
    "derivedFrom": "OBDb/Ford-F-150 @ 7b0146e384 (2026-11-15), signalsets/v3/default.json; OBDb/Ford-Maverick @ 0417358dee (2026-11-15), signalsets/v3/default.json (CC BY-SA 4.0)",
    "confidence": "Imported from OBDb by tools/obdb-import (map: tools/obdb-import/ford-f150-hybrid.map.json). Group (1) is OBDb's F-150 data for 2021+ trucks, unchanged except that the 12 V battery age (days) is shown in hours. Group (2), every HVBAT_* signal, is a guess that the PowerBoost's battery controller answers the same identifiers as its siblings; OBDb has no F-150 capture from 7E4/7EC to confirm or refute it. A read that fails is skipped by the app, so a wrong guess costs a little time per poll and shows nothing. First thing to do with a truck: see which of the 7E4 reads answer, then check the sign of HVBAT_CURRENT (positive must be discharge).",
    "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
  },
  "protocol": {
    "canFormat": "11bit",
    "obdProtocol": 6,
    "note": "ISO 15765-4 CAN 11-bit/500k. Engine computer 7E0 -> 7E8, body 726 -> 72E, cluster 720 -> 728; hybrid battery controller assumed at 7E4 -> 7EC as on Ford's other hybrids."
  },
